Falsifies `capability::derive_never_widens_rights`.

The defect: the two operands swapped inside `Rights::is_subset_of`, so the predicate
answers "does `other` fit inside `self`" while every caller reads it as the opposite.
This is the single most plausible error in a one-line bitwise predicate.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(derived.rights.0 & !src_rights.0, 0)` in
`derive_never_widens_rights`, on a request holding a bit the source lacks.

**This record discriminates where an earlier one did not.** Applied to an older harness,
exactly this diff left ten of the crate's twelve harnesses green, the central theorem among
them, because the theorem asserted
`derived.rights.is_subset_of(src_rights)` while `derive` guards on
`rights.is_subset_of(src.rights)`: one wrong predicate satisfied both sides. The harness
now states the subset relation as `x & !y == 0`, which the implementation does not get
to choose, and this diff turns it red.

The record it replaces was the `CannotWiden` guard deleted from `derive`. The rewritten
harness still catches that (`requested.0 & !src_rights.0` goes red on it); it is replaced
rather than kept because a harness carries one record and the guard-drop was never the
blind spot.

diff --git a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
index 86709e0e..d63c7fca 100644
--- a/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/capability/src/lib.rs
@@ -140,7 +140,7 @@ impl Rights {
 
     /// Is `self` no more than `other`? The subset test that [`CapabilityTable::derive`] turns on.
     pub const fn is_subset_of(self, other: Rights) -> bool {
-        self.0 & !other.0 == 0
+        other.0 & !self.0 == 0
     }
 }
 
